course: biology
lesson: 92
title: The aquarium: jellyfish, octopuses, sea stars and the deep sea
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some of the strangest animals in the aquarium are not fish at all: jellyfish, octopuses, cuttlefish, sea stars and lobsters have no backbone, which makes them invertebrates.
explain: A jellyfish pulses in its blue-lit round tank with no brain, no heart and no bones, yet it swims, catches food and grows, steered by a simple net of nerves.
explain: An octopus is the opposite, a clever animal with a big brain, three hearts and blue blood, and so soft that it can pour itself through a gap no bigger than its beak.
explain: In the dark deep-sea gallery, fish glow with their own light, because far down where sunlight never reaches, making light is the only way to be seen.
explain: Cartoons borrow a few true features, like Patrick's five arms in SpongeBob, and invent the rest, so a good scientist checks which parts are real.
fact: Jellyfish have no brain, heart or bones, are about 95 per cent water, and swim by squeezing their bell; they live in round tanks so they are not trapped in corners.
fact: Jellyfish are not fish: they are relatives of corals and sea anemones, and fish have backbones, gills and fins while jellyfish have none of these.
fact: A small jellyfish called Turritopsis can turn back from an adult into a young stage when hurt or stressed, but it can still be eaten or fall ill, so it is not truly immortal.
fact: An octopus has no bones, and its beak is its only hard part, so it can squeeze through any hole bigger than the beak; octopuses have escaped from aquarium tanks.
fact: Octopuses can learn to unscrew jar lids, even from the inside, and their eight arms are full of nerve cells, so each arm can touch, taste and act partly on its own.
fact: An octopus has three hearts, two for the gills and one for the body, and its blood is blue because it uses copper instead of iron to carry oxygen.
fact: Most octopuses live only one or two years.
fact: Cuttlefish change colour and pattern in less than a second using millions of tiny colour sacs in the skin; they seem to be colour-blind, yet they still match their backgrounds very well.
fact: A sea star opens a mussel by pulling with its tube feet until the mussel tires, then pushes its stomach out of its mouth into the shell.
fact: A sea star has no brain, can regrow a lost arm, and has a simple eye spot at the tip of each arm that senses light.
fact: Live lobsters are usually greenish brown and turn red only when cooked, because heat breaks down a protein that hides the red colour; a rare blue lobster, by a common estimate about one in two million, has a gene change.
fact: Many deep-sea animals make their own light, called bioluminescence, to find food, lure prey and signal; the anglerfish dangles a glowing lure in front of its mouth.
fact: In SpongeBob, Patrick is a sea star and Squidward is named like a squid, but his creator said he is an octopus, drawn with six legs instead of eight arms; a real sea sponge, like SpongeBob's namesake, is a simple animal with no brain that filters water.
exercise: Which of these does a jellyfish have? | A simple net of nerves | A small brain at the top of its bell | A heart that pumps blue blood | Bones inside its soft bell
exercise: Why are jellyfish tanks round? | So the jellies are not trapped in the corners | So the light shines on them better | Because jellyfish can only ever swim round in circles | So visitors can see from every side
exercise: Are jellyfish fish? | No, they are relatives of corals and sea anemones | Yes, they are soft fish that simply have no scales | Yes, they are baby fish | No, they are a kind of plant
exercise: What can the jellyfish Turritopsis do? | Turn back from an adult into a young stage | Live forever with nothing able to harm it | Change into a small fish once it has grown up fully | Grow a new brain each year
exercise: What decides how small a gap an octopus can squeeze through? | Its beak, its only hard part | Its long bendy backbone | Its eyes | Its three hearts
exercise: What can a clever octopus learn to do with a jar? | Unscrew the lid, even from the inside | Break the glass open by tapping it with its beak | Fill it with ink to hide | Roll it to the keeper for food
exercise: Why can an octopus arm act partly on its own? | It is full of nerve cells | It has its own small heart | It has its own eye at the tip | It has a brain in every sucker
exercise: How many hearts does an octopus have? | Three | One | Two | Eight
exercise: Why is octopus blood blue? | It uses copper, not iron, to carry oxygen | It drinks lots of seawater, which is blue | It is full of ink | It is very cold
exercise: How long do most octopuses live? | One or two years | About fifty years | About ten years | A few days
exercise: How fast can a cuttlefish change its colour? | In less than a second | In about an hour or so | Only overnight | Only once a year
exercise: How does a sea star eat a mussel? | It pulls the shell open and pushes its stomach inside | It crushes the shell with its many tiny sharp teeth | It swallows the mussel whole | It sucks the mussel out with a straw-like mouth
exercise: What colour is a live lobster usually? | Greenish brown | Bright tomato red | Bright sky blue | Pure snowy white
exercise: Why does a lobster turn red when cooked? | Heat breaks down a protein that hides the red colour | The hot water is coloured red | It blushes from the heat | Its red blood leaks out into the shell as it cooks
exercise: What is it called when an animal makes its own light? | Bioluminescence | Camouflage | Echolocation | Hibernation
exercise: What does an anglerfish use its glowing lure for? | To attract prey to its mouth | To light up the path ahead as it swims | To keep warm in the cold deep sea | To scare off big hungry sharks
exercise: What kind of animal is Patrick in SpongeBob? | A sea star | A jellyfish | A pink fish | A sea sponge
test: two octopuses in the tank, how many hearts is that | 6 | 2 | 4 | 16
test: An octopus and a sea star share a rock pool. The sea star has five arms. How many arms in all? | 13 | 10 | 15 | 18
test: Squidward is drawn with six legs. How many arms would a real octopus need to add? | Two | Four | None | Six
test: my nan says jellyfish are just fish with no bones, innit? | No, they are not fish at all but cousins of corals | Yes, they are fish that lost their bones long ago | Yes, but only the big ones | No, they are a kind of seaweed
test: What would happen if an octopus tank had a loose lid? | The octopus might squeeze out and wander off | Nothing, because octopuses can never leave the water | The octopus would hide its ink in the gap | The other fish would swim out
test: The keeper says a jellyfish is "immortal". What is the best correction? | It can turn young again but can still be eaten or get ill | It really can never die in any way, not even if eaten | It dies after one day | It lives exactly a hundred years
test: In a cartoon a sea star wears glasses and reads books in its brain. What is true of a real sea star? | It has no brain and has an eye spot at each arm tip | It has two eyes and a big brain | It has a little brain in each arm but no eyes at all | It sees only with glasses
test: SpongeBob is a square yellow kitchen sponge. What is a real sea sponge? | A simple animal with no brain that filters water | A soft green plant that grows on rocks in the sea | A fish that soaks up water | A mineral made by the sea
test: A cartoon lobster is bright red, swimming in the sea. What would you fix? | Live lobsters are greenish brown; red is the cooked colour | Lobsters are always bright blue while they are in the sea | Nothing, lobsters are red alive | Lobsters are white until they grow up
test: Cuttlefish seem to be colour-blind. Which is true? | They still match their backgrounds very well | They cannot change colour at all, so they just stay grey | They only turn black and white | They copy the colours of other fish
test: Why would light be so useful in the deep sea? | No sunlight reaches there, so it is the only way to be seen | It warms the freezing water | It makes green plants grow all over the dark sea floor | It frightens away the sunlight
test: A sea star loses an arm to a crab. What happens next? | It can grow a new arm | It dies at once from the wound | It walks with four arms forever | The crab grows into a sea star
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the jellyfish? | It is an invertebrate allied to corals, devoid of brain, heart and bones | It is a boneless fish possessing gills, fins and a simple brain | It is a marine plant that pulses with the tide | It is the juvenile form of the octopus
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of hearts possessed by three octopuses. | Nine | Three | Six | Twenty-four
exam: Identify the principal reason for which octopus blood is blue in colour. | Oxygen is carried by a substance containing copper rather than iron | The animal ingests large quantities of seawater, which tints the blood | Its ink is dissolved within the blood | Its body temperature is exceedingly low
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colour of the lobster. | It is greenish brown in life and turns red upon cooking | It is red in life and turns brown upon being cooked | It is invariably blue throughout its life | It is white in life and turns red with age
